<ChapterLayout
  title="4.4 — Discovering Exoplanets · AST 100"
  description="How we find worlds we cannot see: the transit method's tell-tale dips in starlight, the radial-velocity wobble read from Doppler-shifted spectra, and direct imaging with a coronagraph — including the real HR 8799 timelapse."
  activeId="4.4" chapter={4} current="/chapter/4/4.4">
  <Hero
    tease="No telescope can simply look at a planet around another star — the star is millions to billions of times brighter. Yet we have found more than 6,300 of them, almost all by indirect tricks: catching the tiny dip when a planet's shadow crosses its star, reading the star's wobble in the colour of its light, and, for a rare few, blotting the star out and photographing the planets themselves."
    client:load />

  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="shadows">Planet Shadows</h2>
      <p>
        Until 1992 every planet known to humanity orbited our own Sun. That year the count began to grow — oddly
        enough, around a dead star, when precise radio timing of a pulsar (<a href={subPath("3.4")}>§3.4</a>) revealed
        two small worlds tugging on it. The real flood, though, came from watching ordinary starlight with
        extraordinary patience. Astronomers now know of <strong>more than 6,300 exoplanets</strong>, and while several
        clever techniques contribute — the pulsar timing just mentioned, <strong>astrometry</strong> (watching a
        star's position shift on the sky), <strong>gravitational microlensing</strong> (a star's gravity briefly
        magnifying a background star, an effect we met with curved spacetime in <a href={subPath("0.1")}>§0.1</a>) —
        the overwhelming majority of discoveries come from just three methods. This lesson walks through them:
        transits, wobbles, and portraits.
      </p>
      <p>
        The champion is the <strong>transit method</strong>. If a planetary system happens to lie edge-on to our line
        of sight, then once per orbit the planet passes directly in front of its star — a <strong>transit</strong> —
        and blocks a tiny, repeating fraction of its light. The fraction tells us the planet's <strong>size</strong>:
        it equals the planet's disk area divided by the star's, so a Jupiter crossing a Sun-like star dims it by about
        <strong>1%</strong>, and an Earth by a mere <strong>0.008%</strong>. The time between dips is the planet's
        <strong>orbital period</strong> — its year. And half an orbit later a second, far shallower dip occurs when
        the planet hides <em>behind</em> the star, taking its own faint glow with it; that
        <strong>secondary eclipse</strong> lets astronomers isolate the planet's own light and take its temperature.
        Run the orbit yourself in the simulator below — drag the slider or use the arrow keys, and try both planet
        sizes.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Transit simulator">
        <TransitPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        What makes transits so powerful is that they scale. A wobble measurement must court one star at a time, but a
        camera can watch a whole field of stars at once and simply wait for dips. NASA's <strong>Kepler</strong> space
        telescope — this chapter's namesake instrument — stared at about <strong>150,000 stars simultaneously</strong>
        from 2009 to 2018 and returned nearly 2,800 confirmed planets, teaching us that planets outnumber stars in
        our galaxy and that the commonest kind is the super-Earth we met in <a href={subPath("4.2")}>§4.2</a>. Its
        successor TESS has been sweeping the whole sky since 2018. The first transiting planet, HD 209458 b in 1999,
        settled any doubt that the wobble detections were real worlds.
      </p>

      <h2 id="wobbles">Wobbles and Portraits</h2>
      <p>
        The second method finds planets by their gravity. A planet does not simply circle a stationary star: star and
        planet both swing around their common balance point, the <strong>barycenter</strong> — think of a heavy adult
        and a small child on a seesaw, whose pivot must sit far closer to the adult. Because the star is thousands of
        times heavier, its share of the motion is tiny — the Sun's answer to Jupiter is a stately wobble of just
        <strong>12.5 metres per second</strong>, bicycle speed — but it is measurable, through the
        <strong>Doppler effect</strong>: the same stretching and squeezing of light waves that revealed the receding
        galaxies in <a href={subPath("2.4")}>§2.4</a>. As the star swings away from us, its light shifts slightly
        toward the red; swinging toward us, toward the blue. A <strong>spectrograph</strong> — the
        rainbow-spreading instrument from <a href={subPath("0.4")}>§0.4</a> — reads the shift in the star's dark
        absorption lines, cycling back and forth with the orbit. Watch the whole mechanism run in the figure below.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Radial-velocity simulator">
        <RVPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        This <strong>radial-velocity method</strong> (radial = along our line of sight) delivered the breakthrough:
        in 1995 Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz measured the four-day wobble of the star 51 Pegasi — the first planet
        ever found around a Sun-like star, and a Nobel Prize in 2019. The wobble's rhythm gives the planet's orbital
        period, and its size gives the planet's <strong>mass</strong> — strictly a minimum mass, since a tilted orbit
        hides some of the motion. Pair a wobble mass with a transit size and you get density, which is precisely how
        the composition chart of <a href={subPath("4.2")}>§4.2</a> was built. Modern spectrographs read wobbles down
        to a walking pace — around one metre per second.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third method is the most direct and the most demanding: actually <strong>photographing</strong> the
        planet. The obstacle is glare — a star outshines its planets by factors of <strong>millions to
        billions</strong>. The cure is a <strong>coronagraph</strong>: a precisely engineered mask inside the
        telescope that blots out the star's disk, creating an artificial eclipse so the faint sparks beside it can
        register. It works best for the planets transits and wobbles miss: <strong>young, massive giants orbiting
        far from their stars</strong>, in systems we view more nearly face-on, where the planets stay clear of the
        glare for their whole orbit. The showcase is <strong>HR 8799</strong>: in 2008 Christian Marois and colleagues
        imaged three giant planets around it at once — the first planetary system ever photographed — adding a fourth
        in 2010. Years of follow-up images, assembled into a famous timelapse, show all four visibly crawling along
        their orbits. Toggle the coronagraph in the figure below, then compare with the real thing underneath. Best of
        all, a direct image captures the planet's <em>own light</em> — light that can be spread into a spectrum and
        searched for the fingerprints of water, methane, and more, the doorway to chapter 5's question of chemistry
        and life.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Direct imaging: HR 8799">
        <ImagingPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <hr />

      <p><em>A shadow, a wobble, a masked portrait — three tricks that turned "are there other worlds?" from
        philosophy into a catalogue of 6,300 and counting. The Planetary Age has built the stage: worlds of rock and
        water at comfortable distances from steady stars. What happens on such a stage — oceans, air, chemistry,
        life — is the Chemical Age, next.</em></p>
    </div>
  </article>

